// NIELSEN PROPOSITION 1 — the Archimedean integrals have no uniform bound.
//
// The paper's §2. Three parts, and they are of genuinely different kinds, which is why they
// are separated here rather than bundled:
//
//   (a) ALGEBRA. The measure and weight combine: t^{-1/2}/|1-t| dt = t^{1/2}/(t-1) dt/t for
//       t > 1. Nielsen states this as "Indeed, ...". Pure computation.
//
//   (b) ANALYSIS. Fatou's lemma on nonnegative integrands, plus the fact that t^{-z-1/2} has
//       a positive minimum c on [1, 1+delta], gives liminf A_n >= c * int_1^{1+delta}
//       dt/(t-1) = +infinity. This is where the real content is, and it is CITED: this
//       formalization has no measures, no integrals, no limits.
//
//   (c) LOGIC. From "the sequence exceeds every real" conclude "no real bounds the sequence
//       at every index". This is Nielsen's "In particular", and it is the one part that is
//       PROVED here, from the definitions of the two notions plus strict-order asymmetry.
//
// WHAT THE SPLIT BUYS. A reader can see exactly which of Nielsen's three moves is checked
// and which are granted. (c) is the step most likely to hide a quantifier error — "tends to
// infinity" and "has no uniform bound" are not interchangeable in general, and the paper
// moves between them in one phrase — so it is the one worth machine-checking. (a) and (b)
// are standard and quotable.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the objects
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// a family {phi_n} of test-function approximants, indexed by n
sort TestFunctionFamily

/// an index n of the approximating sequence
sort Index

/// a real quantity — the value of an integral, or a candidate bound. Opaque: this file does
/// not model the reals, it models the ORDER relation the argument uses.
sort Magnitude

/// the Archimedean integral A_n at index n, for the family: the quantity Nielsen shows
/// diverges. Up to a fixed positive factor this is
/// int_1^{1+delta} phi_n(t) t^{-1/2}/(t-1) dt.
oper theArchimedeanIntegral(p: TestFunctionFamily, n: Index) => Magnitude

/// strict order on magnitudes
pred below(x: Magnitude, y: Magnitude)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the properties
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// the family is Masullo's: phi_n = h * (rho_eps * phi_z) with a NONNEGATIVE mollifier,
/// h supported in (1, infinity) and valued in [0,1], approaching the indicator, and
/// phi_n(t) -> t^{-z} pointwise for t > 1, at some admissible real z > max{1, sup I}.
pred isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p: TestFunctionFamily)

/// every A_n lies below this candidate bound. Long-form (declaration + axiom) because a
/// definition clause cannot infer `n`'s sort under the quantifier — 2b4m PAINPOINTS 16, same
/// cause as the two axioms in shared/vocabulary.b4m. This SAYS WHAT boundedBy MEANS.
pred boundedBy(p: TestFunctionFamily, b: Magnitude)
axiom boundedByMeansEveryValueIsBelow:
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily; forall b: Magnitude;
  boundedBy(p, b) iff (forall n: Index; below(theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n), b))

/// SOME candidate bounds every A_n — "the asserted uniform upper bound" exists
pred hasAUniformBound(p: TestFunctionFamily)

/// EVERY candidate is exceeded at some index — the operative content of liminf A_n = +infinity
pred exceedsEveryBound(p: TestFunctionFamily)

/// the family defeats the uniform-boundedness assertion
pred theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p: TestFunctionFamily):
  theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p) iff (not hasAUniformBound(p)).

/// Montel's theorem applies to the family, in the form §1.4.3 uses: a subsequence converges
/// to a finite holomorphic limit
pred montelApplies(p: TestFunctionFamily)

intheory theMasulloFamilyHasNoUniformBound
intheory divergenceDefeatsAUniformBound

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (a) ALGEBRA — the measure/weight identity. Nielsen's "Indeed, ...".
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The Archimedean measure and the trace-formula weight combine to the stated integrand.
/// `t^{-1/2}/|1-t| dt = t^{1/2}/(t-1) * dt/t` for `t > 1`.
hole theMeasureAndWeightCombine
  cites "standard: elementary algebra of the integrand — for t > 1, |1 - t| = t - 1, so t^{-1/2}/(t-1) = (t^{1/2}/(t-1)) * (1/t). Checked symbolically here (sympy: simplify(a - b) == 0). The weight 1/|1 - g_v| really is the one in the manuscript's §1.4.3 display at txt 1265, and reappears in Thm 12.4.1.1 at txt 31136":
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) ->
  theIntegrandIsTheStatedOne(p)

/// the integrand of A_n is the one Nielsen displays
pred theIntegrandIsTheStatedOne(p: TestFunctionFamily)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (b) ANALYSIS — Fatou, and the logarithmic singularity. CITED, not proved.
//
// This is the load-bearing analytic step, and the honest position is that this
// formalization cannot check it: there are no measures, no integrals and no limits at this
// altitude. What a reader must grant is stated as precisely as possible, with sources.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Fatou's lemma applies, because a nonnegative mollifier makes every phi_n nonnegative.
///
/// NOTE THE HINGE. Fatou needs nonnegative integrands. Nielsen states "use a nonnegative
/// mollifier" as a HYPOTHESIS, and Masullo's §1.4.2 construction h*(rho_eps * phi_z) with h
/// valued in [0,1] appears to satisfy it — but whether Masullo's own rho_eps is nonnegative
/// is NOT traced in either document, and if he intended a signed mollifier this step needs
/// rework. This is the narrowest place the objection could fail.
hole fatouAppliesToTheNonnegativeApproximants
  cites "standard: Fatou's lemma — liminf int f_n >= int liminf f_n for nonnegative measurable f_n. Rudin, Real and Complex Analysis Thm 1.28; Folland, Real Analysis Thm 2.15. CONDITIONAL on the approximants being nonnegative, which Nielsen takes as a hypothesis ('use a nonnegative mollifier') and which this formalization does not trace to Masullo's own rho_eps":
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) -> theIntegrandIsTheStatedOne(p) ->
  theLiminfIsAtLeastTheLimitIntegral(p)

/// liminf A_n is bounded below by the integral of the pointwise liminf
pred theLiminfIsAtLeastTheLimitIntegral(p: TestFunctionFamily)

/// The limiting integral diverges: t^{-z-1/2} has a positive minimum c on [1, 1+delta], and
/// c * int_1^{1+delta} dt/(t-1) = +infinity — a logarithmic singularity at t = 1.
///
/// This is the mathematical core of the objection, and it is elementary: the integrand is
/// asymptotically c/(t-1) and that integral diverges. Checked symbolically here, but as an
/// isolated fact about 1/(t-1), not as a statement about the manuscript's measure.
hole theLimitingIntegralDiverges
  cites "standard: int_1^{1+delta} dt/(t-1) = +infinity (a logarithmic singularity), and a continuous positive function on a compact interval attains a positive minimum — any real-analysis text, e.g. Rudin, Principles of Mathematical Analysis Thm 4.16 (minimum) and §6 (improper integrals). Checked symbolically here: the limiting integrand t^{-z-1/2}/(t-1) satisfies lim_{t->1} (t-1)*f(t) = 1, and integrate(1/(t-1), (t, 1, 1+delta)) = oo":
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  theLiminfIsAtLeastTheLimitIntegral(p) -> exceedsEveryBound(p)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the definitional facts the logic step needs. Declared + axiomatized because a definition
// clause cannot infer a bound variable's sort under a quantifier (2b4m PAINPOINTS 16) —
// these SAY WHAT THE PREDICATES MEAN and assume nothing about the mathematics.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// a uniform bound is some candidate bounding every index
axiom aUniformBoundIsSomeBound:
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  hasAUniformBound(p) iff (exists b: Magnitude; boundedBy(p, b))

/// exceeding every bound: each candidate is beaten at some index
axiom exceedingMeansEachCandidateIsBeaten:
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  exceedsEveryBound(p) iff
  (forall b: Magnitude; exists n: Index; below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n)))

/// strict order is asymmetric — nothing is below itself
hole belowIsIrreflexive
  cites "standard: a strict order is irreflexive — immediate from asymmetry, which `below` has as the strict order on the reals. Any analysis or order-theory text":
  forall x: Magnitude; not below(x, x)

/// and transitive
hole belowIsTransitive
  cites "standard: the strict order on the reals is transitive — any analysis text":
  forall x: Magnitude; forall y: Magnitude; forall z: Magnitude;
  below(x, y) -> below(y, z) -> below(x, z)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (c) THE LOGIC — Nielsen's "In particular". PROVED.
//
// "The sequence A_n tends to +infinity. In particular, the asserted uniform upper bound does
// not exist." Those are different statements and the paper joins them in two words, so this
// is the step worth checking: if every candidate is beaten somewhere, no candidate bounds
// everywhere. The proof assumes a uniform bound, unpacks it to a witness b, beats b at some
// index n, and derives below(b, b) — contradicting irreflexivity.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Strategy: assume a bound, unpack its witness, beat it, contradict irreflexivity.
theorem divergenceDefeatsAUniformBound:
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  exceedsEveryBound(p) -> (not hasAUniformBound(p))
proof
  @generalize-p |
    fix p: TestFunctionFamily {
      @given-exceeds |
        assume exceedsEveryBound(p) {
          @exceeds |
            exceedsEveryBound(p)
            [by hypothesis given-exceeds]
          @exceeding-unfolds |
            forall q: TestFunctionFamily;
              exceedsEveryBound(q) iff
              (forall b: Magnitude; exists n: Index;
              below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(q, n)))
            [by cite exceedingMeansEachCandidateIsBeaten]
          @exceeding-at-p |
            exceedsEveryBound(p) iff
              (forall b: Magnitude; exists n: Index;
              below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n)))
            [by apply_at(p) exceeding-unfolds]
          @each-candidate-is-beaten |
            forall b: Magnitude; exists n: Index;
              below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n))
            [using tautology exceeding-at-p exceeds]
          @given-a-uniform-bound |
            assume hasAUniformBound(p) {
              @has-a-bound |
                hasAUniformBound(p)
                [by hypothesis given-a-uniform-bound]
              @uniform-unfolds |
                forall q: TestFunctionFamily;
                  hasAUniformBound(q) iff (exists b: Magnitude; boundedBy(q, b))
                [by cite aUniformBoundIsSomeBound]
              @uniform-at-p |
                hasAUniformBound(p) iff (exists b: Magnitude; boundedBy(p, b))
                [by apply_at(p) uniform-unfolds]
              @a-bound-exists |
                exists b: Magnitude; boundedBy(p, b)
                [using tautology uniform-at-p has-a-bound]
              @bounded-definition |
                forall q: TestFunctionFamily; forall c: Magnitude;
                  boundedBy(q, c) iff
                  (forall n: Index; below(theArchimedeanIntegral(q, n), c))
                [by cite boundedByMeansEveryValueIsBelow]
              @irreflexive |
                forall x: Magnitude; not below(x, x)
                [by cite belowIsIrreflexive]
              @transitive |
                forall x: Magnitude; forall y: Magnitude; forall z: Magnitude;
                  below(x, y) -> below(y, z) -> below(x, z)
                [by cite belowIsTransitive]
              @no-bound-from-the-witness |
                unpack b: Magnitude from a-bound-exists {
                  @bounded-by-b |
                    boundedBy(p, b)
                    [by hypothesis no-bound-from-the-witness]
                  @bounded-at-p-b |
                    boundedBy(p, b) iff
                      (forall n: Index; below(theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n), b))
                    [by apply_at(p, b) bounded-definition]
                  @every-value-is-below-b |
                    forall n: Index; below(theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n), b)
                    [using tautology bounded-at-p-b bounded-by-b]
                  @b-is-beaten |
                    exists n: Index; below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n))
                    [by apply_at(b) each-candidate-is-beaten]
                  @no-bound-from-the-index |
                    unpack n: Index from b-is-beaten {
                      @b-below-the-value |
                        below(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n))
                        [by hypothesis no-bound-from-the-index]
                      @the-value-below-b |
                        below(theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n), b)
                        [by apply_at(n) every-value-is-below-b]
                      @b-below-itself |
                        below(b, b)
                        [using specialize transitive(b, theArchimedeanIntegral(p, n), b)
                          b-below-the-value the-value-below-b]
                      @b-not-below-itself |
                        not below(b, b)
                        [by apply_at(b) irreflexive]
                      @conclusion-no-uniform-bound |
                        not hasAUniformBound(p)
                        [by ex_falso b-below-itself b-not-below-itself]
                    }
                  @conclusion-no-bound-at-b |
                    not hasAUniformBound(p)
                    [by unpacked no-bound-from-the-index]
                }
              @no-uniform-bound-after-all |
                not hasAUniformBound(p)
                [by unpacked no-bound-from-the-witness]
            }
          @uniform-bound-is-self-refuting |
            hasAUniformBound(p) -> (not hasAUniformBound(p))
            [by discharge given-a-uniform-bound]
          @conclusion-inner |
            not hasAUniformBound(p)
            [using tautology uniform-bound-is-self-refuting]
        }
      @exceeds-implies-no-bound |
        exceedsEveryBound(p) -> (not hasAUniformBound(p))
        [by discharge given-exceeds]
    }
  @conclusion |
    forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
      exceedsEveryBound(p) -> (not hasAUniformBound(p))
    [by generalize generalize-p]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PROPOSITION 1, assembled: algebra, then Fatou, then divergence, then the logic.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Nielsen Proposition 1. Strategy: chain (a) the integrand identity, (b) Fatou and the
/// divergence of the limiting integral, then (c) the proved logical step.
theorem theMasulloFamilyHasNoUniformBound:
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) ->
  theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p)
proof
  @generalize-p |
    fix p: TestFunctionFamily {
      @given-is-the-family |
        assume isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) {
          @is-the-family |
            isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p)
            [by hypothesis given-is-the-family]
          @the-integrand-is-the-stated-one |
            theIntegrandIsTheStatedOne(p)
            [using specialize theMeasureAndWeightCombine(p) is-the-family]
          @fatou-gives-the-lower-bound |
            theLiminfIsAtLeastTheLimitIntegral(p)
            [using specialize fatouAppliesToTheNonnegativeApproximants(p)
              is-the-family the-integrand-is-the-stated-one]
          @the-sequence-exceeds-every-bound |
            exceedsEveryBound(p)
            [using specialize theLimitingIntegralDiverges(p)
              fatou-gives-the-lower-bound]
          @no-uniform-bound |
            not hasAUniformBound(p)
            [using specialize divergenceDefeatsAUniformBound(p)
              the-sequence-exceeds-every-bound]
          @no-uniform-bound-definition |
            forall q: TestFunctionFamily;
              theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(q) iff
              (not hasAUniformBound(q))
            [by definition(0) theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound]
          @no-uniform-bound-at-p |
            theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p) iff
              (not hasAUniformBound(p))
            [by apply_at(p) no-uniform-bound-definition]
          @conclusion-no-uniform-bound |
            theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p)
            [using tautology no-uniform-bound-at-p no-uniform-bound]
        }
      @family-has-no-bound |
        isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) ->
          theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p)
        [by discharge given-is-the-family]
    }
  @conclusion |
    forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
      isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p) ->
      theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p)
    [by generalize generalize-p]
qed

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT MONTEL REQUIRES. Nielsen's Corollary 1 rests on this, and it is the manuscript's own
// usage: §1.4.3 invokes Montel to extract a convergent subsequence with a finite
// holomorphic limit, which needs local uniform boundedness.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LINK TO THE MANUSCRIPT. Everything above is CONDITIONAL on
// `isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p)`. Without a claim that Masullo's §1.4.2 family actually
// satisfies that predicate, the chain proves nothing about his proof — the same vacuity that
// an audit found in grh.b4m's GRH statement, where opaque predicates made the conclusion
// independent of its antecedents.
//
// So the link is stated explicitly, and it is the one place Nielsen's objection touches the
// manuscript's text rather than classical analysis. It carries TWO claims, and the second is
// the hinge the whole objection turns on.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// (i) THE SHAPE. Masullo's §1.4.2 construction has the form Prop 1 needs: phi_{h,eps,z}(t)
/// = h(t)(rho_eps * phi_z)(t), with h a bump function supported over (1, +infinity) and
/// valued in [0,1], tending pointwise monotonically to the indicator, and phi_z(t) = t^{-z}.
/// All of this is his own text, verbatim.
hole masullosConstructionHasTheStatedShape
  cites "standard: §1.4.2 (txt 1326-1337) states the construction verbatim — 'take eps > 0 small, and rho_eps the resulting mollifier as a function of t in R_{>0}. Take h(t) in D(R_{>0}) be a bump function supported over (1, +infinity) only (and valued in [0, 1])', then 'phi_{h,eps,z}(t) := h(t)(rho_eps * phi_z)' which 'tends to phi_z(t) pointwise (for t != 1) as eps -> 0 and h -> 1_{(1,+infinity)} pointwise, as h ... remains valued in [0, 1] and below 1_{(1,+infinity)}, converging pointwise monotonically'. Repeated at txt 2783-2788":
  theMasulloShapeIsAsStated()

/// (ii) NONNEGATIVITY, which is what Fatou requires.
///
/// THIS WAS THE OBJECTION'S ONE SOFT SPOT, AND MASULLO'S OWN WORDS CLOSE IT. Nielsen writes
/// "Use a nonnegative mollifier", which reads as an added hypothesis — and an earlier version
/// of this file recorded it as an `assertion:` about his unstated intent for that reason.
/// Re-reading §1.4.2 settles it the other way:
///
///   - he calls rho_eps a MOLLIFIER, which is a term of art: nonnegative, smooth, compactly
///     supported, unit mass. He never redefines it, so the standard meaning governs.
///   - h is explicitly "valued in [0, 1]", hence nonnegative.
///   - phi_z(t) = t^{-z} is positive for t > 1, and zero on (0,1).
///
/// A convolution of nonnegative functions is nonnegative, and a product of nonnegative
/// functions is nonnegative. So the approximants are nonnegative BY HIS CONSTRUCTION, and
/// Nielsen's hypothesis is not an extra assumption — it is a consequence of the text.
///
/// BUT BE PRECISE ABOUT THE RESIDUAL. This is forced only CONDITIONAL ON the standard
/// meaning of a term he uses and never defines. He writes "the resulting mollifier" with no
/// definition anywhere in 940 pages (grep: the word appears 3 times, never defined). The
/// term-of-art reading is the only defensible one and nothing in his text contradicts it, so
/// this is a `standard:` citation about mathematical usage — not an `assertion:` about his
/// private intent, and not a certainty either. If he meant a signed kernel, the word choice
/// was misleading and Fatou's applicability would need rework.
///
/// THE OTHER HYPOTHESIS NIELSEN NEEDS is pointwise convergence phi_n(t) -> t^{-z} for t > 1,
/// and that one is verbatim his: "tends to phi_z(t) pointwise (for t != 1)" (txt 1336).
///
/// A WORRY CHECKED AND DISMISSED: each phi_n vanishes on a neighbourhood of t = 1 (h is
/// supported in (1, +infinity) and approaches the indicator from below), so one might fear
/// Fatou is being applied with the quantifiers swapped. It is not. Nielsen fixes delta FIRST
/// and applies Fatou over n at that fixed delta; at each fixed t > 1 the pointwise limit is
/// t^{-z} > 0, so the shrinking neighbourhoods do not depress the liminf. No swap occurs.
hole aMollifierConstructionIsNonnegative
  cites "standard: a MOLLIFIER is by definition nonnegative, smooth, compactly supported with unit mass — Hormander, The Analysis of Linear Partial Differential Operators I §1.3; Evans, PDE Appendix C.5; Folland, Real Analysis §8.2. Masullo calls rho_eps a mollifier (txt 1329) and never redefines the term. His h is 'valued in [0, 1]' (txt 1330) and phi_z(t) = t^{-z} > 0 for t > 1. A convolution of nonnegative functions is nonnegative and a product of nonnegative functions is nonnegative, so phi_{h,eps,z} >= 0 follows from HIS construction, not from an added hypothesis":
  theMasulloShapeIsAsStated() -> theApproximantsAreNonnegative()

/// the construction has the §1.4.2 shape
pred theMasulloShapeIsAsStated()
/// and its approximants are nonnegative, so Fatou applies
pred theApproximantsAreNonnegative()

intheory masullosFamilyIsOfThisKind

/// Masullo's family is one Prop 1 applies to: the shape is his, and nonnegativity follows
/// from the standard meaning of "mollifier" plus the sign of his own factors.
theorem masullosFamilyIsOfThisKind:
  exists p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p)
proof
  @the-shape-is-as-stated |
    theMasulloShapeIsAsStated()
    [by cite masullosConstructionHasTheStatedShape]
  @nonnegativity-follows |
    theMasulloShapeIsAsStated() -> theApproximantsAreNonnegative()
    [by cite aMollifierConstructionIsNonnegative]
  @the-approximants-are-nonnegative |
    theApproximantsAreNonnegative()
    [by modus_ponens nonnegativity-follows the-shape-is-as-stated]
  @shape-and-sign-give-the-family |
    theMasulloShapeIsAsStated() -> theApproximantsAreNonnegative() ->
      (exists p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p))
    [by cite theShapeAndSignDetermineTheFamily]
  @needs-the-sign |
    theApproximantsAreNonnegative() ->
      (exists p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p))
    [by modus_ponens shape-and-sign-give-the-family the-shape-is-as-stated]
  @conclusion |
    exists p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p)
    [by modus_ponens needs-the-sign the-approximants-are-nonnegative]
qed

/// and that those two properties are exactly what `isTheMasulloApproximantFamily` asks for.
/// This is the one modelling bridge: it says the opaque predicate means what its doc comment
/// says it means.
hole theShapeAndSignDetermineTheFamily
  cites "standard: this is the definition of `isTheMasulloApproximantFamily` unfolded — a family of the §1.4.2 shape whose approximants are nonnegative IS a family of the kind Prop 1 applies to, since those are the only two properties the proposition uses (the shape gives the integrand, the sign gives Fatou). Stated rather than written as a definition block because the predicate is declared over an opaque sort with no constructor":
  theMasulloShapeIsAsStated() -> theApproximantsAreNonnegative() ->
  (exists p: TestFunctionFamily; isTheMasulloApproximantFamily(p))

hole montelNeedsAUniformBound
  cites "standard: Montel's theorem — a family of holomorphic functions locally uniformly bounded on a domain is relatively compact, so has a locally uniformly convergent subsequence. The boundedness hypothesis is not removable: without it no finite holomorphic limit is obtained. Conway, Functions of One Complex Variable Thm VII.2.9; Rudin, Real and Complex Analysis Thm 14.6. The manuscript applies it at §1.4.3 and §1.8.4.3":
  forall p: TestFunctionFamily;
  montelApplies(p) -> (not theArchimedeanIntegralsHaveNoUniformBound(p))
